# Engineering Standards — [FILL IN: App Name]

These rules apply to **every** change in this repository — a one-line
tweak and a multi-file feature carry the same bar. Read `ARCHITECTURE.md`
before touching code; it explains the layering these rules assume.

> Adapted from the `iOSAppStarterKit` starter kit — see that kit's
> `LESSONS_LEARNED.md` for the specific incidents that motivated several
> of these rules (particularly Device support and the pre-merge doc/
> memory check).

## Default to production-grade, regardless of the repo's name or stage

If this repo's name, folder location, or early commit history suggests
"prototype"/"POC"/"early-stage" (e.g. a leftover working-title
directory name from before the app had a real identity), **do not infer
the app's actual maturity or stakes from that naming** — ask, or default
to treating it as production-bound. A leftover "POC" directory name is
easy to keep long after an app is real and actively shipping to real
users; assuming naming implies license to cut corners is a mistake, not
a safe default. Concretely: don't use "it's early-stage" as a reason to
skip error handling, edge cases, live/mid-session enforcement, or tests
— default to the more robust option when two designs are in tension,
unless told otherwise.

## The standard to write against

Before finishing any change, ask: *would a Senior Staff iOS engineer,
reading this cold, call it clean — or would they say "this needs a
rewrite before I'd take it over"?* The target is that any iOS developer
can be handed this repository at any time and understand it, and safely
extend it, in a reasonable amount of time with no verbal handoff. That
means:
- No dead code. If a property, parameter, or type isn't read/used
  anywhere, delete it — don't leave it "in case."
- No God objects. If a View or ViewModel starts doing rendering *and*
  business logic *and* state-machine orchestration, that's the signal to
  extract — View renders, ViewModel owns state + decisions.
- No silent architecture violations. `Presentation/` depends on `Domain/`
  protocols, never directly on `Data/` concrete types, except at the one
  composition root (`[FILL IN: App]App.swift` / `[FILL IN: root view]
  .init`). If a new external dependency (network, disk, a new system
  framework) is needed, it gets a protocol in `Domain/Protocols/` and a
  concrete implementation in `Data/` — see `ARCHITECTURE.md`'s protocol
  list for the pattern. No exceptions for "it's just a small thing."
- No unexplained magic numbers or arbitrary thresholds — if a value like a
  timing constant, threshold, or animation parameter isn't self-evident,
  say in one line why that value, not just what it is.
- Consistent naming: protocols read as capabilities (gerund/verb-ing,
  e.g. `AudioRecording`, `RemoteConfiguring`), concrete types are named
  for their concrete technology (e.g. `AudioRecorder`,
  `FirebaseRemoteConfigService`). Follow this for anything new.

Equally important — **don't over-engineer past this bar either**. A
senior reviewer criticizes unnecessary layers and speculative abstraction
just as readily as sloppiness. See "Why not more layers" in
`ARCHITECTURE.md`. Abstract exactly the boundaries that benefit (things
you'd realistically swap or fake in a test), and stop there. If you're
adding a protocol with exactly one implementation and no plausible
second one or test need, that's a smell in the other direction.

## Device / platform support

[FILL IN: which platforms this app targets — iPhone only, Universal
(iPhone+iPad), watchOS, macOS, visionOS, some combination]

Every feature and UI change supports **every targeted platform from the
first version that ships it** — not platform-A-first with platform-B as
a later retrofit. (This lesson comes from a real app that added iPad support as a retrofit onto
an already-iPhone-only app; it took several rounds and caused a real
iPhone regression along the way — see `LESSONS_LEARNED.md` items #3–#4
in the starter kit. This rule exists specifically to avoid repeating
that.)
- Any behavior that's genuinely specific to one device/platform (not
  just to an orientation, size class, or measured geometry) must be
  gated on an explicit device-idiom check (`UIDevice.current
  .userInterfaceIdiom` on iOS/iPadOS, or the equivalent platform-detection
  API elsewhere) — never inferred from a geometry/orientation signal
  like a `GeometryReader`-derived compact/regular check, even where that
  signal happens to correlate with the target device most of the time.
- Design and verify layout/behavior for every targeted platform up
  front.
- Test coverage must include every device/platform path wherever logic
  actually branches on it — assert both branches explicitly, not just
  whichever the simulator/default happens to exercise.
- Manual/visual verification covers every targeted platform before a UI
  change is called done.

## App Store subscription compliance

[FILL IN: delete this whole section if the app has no subscription/IAP.
Otherwise keep it — these are real App Review rejection reasons, not
hypothetical.] See `docs/subscription-paywall-pattern.md`'s "App Store
Review requirements for the purchase flow" and `LESSONS_LEARNED.md` #16
for the incident this came from. Standing rules for any subscription/
paywall change:
- The subscription's title, length, price, and functional Privacy
  Policy/Terms of Use links must be visible **directly in the purchase
  flow view itself**, not just reachable elsewhere in the app.
- The purchase flow must be reachable **without needing to exhaust any
  usage limit first** — a settings-level "Subscribe" entry point (or
  equivalent), not only a reactive paywall.
- If the App Store Description references the Terms of Use, it must
  contain an actual clickable URL, not prose referencing "our Terms" —
  required whenever the app uses Apple's Standard EULA (no Custom EULA
  configured), since then the Description itself is the only place that
  link can live.
- The App Store Description must not misrepresent data handling (e.g.
  claiming data "never leaves your device" when a third-party API is
  actually called) — see `docs/legal-compliance-checklist.md`.

## Before writing code

For anything beyond a trivial, obviously-scoped fix: **plan first, get
explicit sign-off, then implement.** This isn't optional process — it's
what "assume a Senior Staff reviewer" means operationally: think through
the design, name the tradeoff, before typing. Use plan mode. State root
causes, not just symptoms, when fixing a bug.

## Testing

- Every non-trivial piece of decision logic (state transitions, data
  transformations, business rules — the kind of thing that used to be
  impossible to test when it lived inside a SwiftUI `body`) gets a unit
  test in `[FILL IN: App]Tests/`, using fakes for every Domain protocol
  (see `ARCHITECTURE.md`'s Testing section for the pattern).
- Changing existing logic means updating or adding tests in the same
  change — never leave tests silently stale or, worse, delete a test
  instead of fixing what it caught.
- `xcodebuild test` must pass before a change is considered done. Not
  "should probably pass" — actually run it.
- Not everything needs a test (a pure layout tweak doesn't), but if
  you're unsure whether something is "logic" or "just view code," err
  toward testing it.
- Any logic that branches on device idiom (per "Device / platform
  support" above) gets tests covering every branch.
- **Test-helper footgun** (see `LESSONS_LEARNED.md` #11): any test
  helper constructing a type with an injectable dependency (credential
  store, analytics, remote config, etc.) must either have no default
  value or default to a **fake**, never to the real, side-effecting
  implementation. A default that silently falls back to the real
  Keychain/network/etc. will eventually cause cross-test contamination
  that's expensive to trace.

## Documentation

- `///` doc comments on every new type, protocol, and non-obvious method
  — explain *why* a seam exists, not just what a method does. This
  project deliberately opts out of a general "minimal comments" default:
  hand-off readiness is the explicit goal here.
- `ARCHITECTURE.md` gets updated whenever the layering, a major type's
  responsibility, or the composition root changes — it must stay accurate
  or it actively misleads the next person who reads it first. Fix it in
  the *same PR* as the code change that made it stale — "a follow-up
  cleanup PR" reliably never happens. See "Before merging a PR" below for
  the enforcement point.
- `README.md` gets updated whenever setup/run/test instructions change.
